# Every screen of the ADE, opened and read by Svatah (T11.4, REQ-SELF-1).
#
# > flows covering every ADE screen's Validate items of Phases 9 and 10 through
# > the AX adapter
#
# One story per screen, and each one is the same three things a person does:
# reach it, see that it is the screen it should be, and see a control that only
# that screen has. The rail reaches eight; the command palette reaches the four
# that are not on it (LLD §13.7's information architecture, T10.3).
#
# ## What these are the *other side* of
#
# `apps/ade/test/shell.spec.ts` has a Playwright case per screen — "flows opens
# and every control on it is named and id'd" and its eleven siblings — and
# `evals/self/checks.yaml` pairs them by check id. The two drive the same
# packaged application through different oracles: this one through the
# accessibility tree macOS publishes, that one through the DOM. A disagreement
# is a finding, and the ids are how the gate knows which two answers to compare.
#
# The phrases are the desktop grounding cases' own, read out of the real ADE by
# `pnpm grounding:desktop-cases`, and the bindings are seeded from the
# `automationId`s by `node scripts/seed-self-bindings.mjs` — no model, no
# recording (LLD §13.9).
#
# ## The number in the file name
#
# `svatah run` executes a project's `test:` blocks in file order, and one
# session serves the whole run — so a flow that ends with `Quit the app` has to
# be the last one, or every flow after it drives an application that is not
# there. `99-` says that out loud; `01-` and `02-` say the rest may run in any
# order.

# The project is opened once, by the first story, through the ADE's own Recent
# list — the same gesture a person makes. Every story after it is on a window
# that already has one.
story: the Flows screen opens and shows its own controls
  The first recent project should be visible
  Click the first recent project
  The Flows rail item should be visible
  Click the Flows rail item
  The Flows heading should be visible
  The Save flow button should be visible

story: the Runs screen opens and shows its own controls
  Click the Runs rail item
  The status: all button should be visible

story: the Bindings screen opens and shows its own controls
  Click the Bindings rail item
  The Verify all bindings button should be visible

story: the Agents screen opens and shows its own controls
  Click the Agents and tools rail item
  The Start the tool server button should be visible

story: the API screen opens and shows its own controls
  Click the API rail item
  The Send request button should be visible

story: the Data screen opens and shows its own controls
  Click the Data rail item
  The Save data button should be visible

story: the Import screen opens and shows its own controls
  Click the Import prototype database rail item
  The Import the database button should be visible

story: the Settings screen opens and shows its own controls
  Click the Settings rail item
  The Where this came from heading should be visible

test: the ADE's eight rail screens
  the Flows screen opens and shows its own controls
  the Runs screen opens and shows its own controls
  the Bindings screen opens and shows its own controls
  the Agents screen opens and shows its own controls
  the API screen opens and shows its own controls
  the Data screen opens and shows its own controls
  the Import screen opens and shows its own controls
  the Settings screen opens and shows its own controls
